Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Israel junction conditions", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9089087495152893`*^9, 3.908908754891224*^9}, {
  3.959442443324087*^9, 3.959442447158428*^9}, {3.9627605304460564`*^9, 
  3.9627605400118885`*^9}, {3.963516145809803*^9, 3.963516145809803*^9}, {
  3.963861639773403*^9, 3.9638616502865086`*^9}, {3.984525992415926*^9, 
  3.9845259961814613`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"56426759-5fd2-ec40-b83a-\
e3e80dc0c163"],

Cell["Function of this program", "Subtitle",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9089089880357375`*^9, 
  3.9089089910285892`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"20a85a00-0216-c74a-9109-\
eaaa08bd46d5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "1. Derive the junction conditions across an interface within a fluid star \
under polar deformation [Ref: ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Lindblom and Detweiler 1985",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1985ApJ...292...12D/abstract"], 
    None},
  ButtonNote->
   "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1985ApJ...292...12D/abstract"],
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " ]."
}], "Subsubtitle",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9089088271798067`*^9, 3.9089088360863085`*^9}, {
   3.9089089231974597`*^9, 3.908908965623436*^9}, {3.959442453276087*^9, 
   3.959442463966982*^9}, {3.962760549811142*^9, 3.962760569311924*^9}, {
   3.9638616557027416`*^9, 3.963861671434618*^9}, {3.9843312878202267`*^9, 
   3.984331313479849*^9}, {3.9843321166998577`*^9, 3.984332132529297*^9}, {
   3.9843323912123985`*^9, 3.984332391822052*^9}, 3.9843355698146496`*^9, {
   3.9844283768859653`*^9, 3.984428432101637*^9}, 3.984526010531494*^9, {
   3.9845274350348263`*^9, 
   3.9845275221538677`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"35e9c1b2-23a1-8944-90e5-\
13af27329e5d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The normal vector of the hypersurface of an interface (e.g. a surface with \
a density jump) within the deformed star is useful for deriving the junction \
conditions that relate variables between the two sides. Below are the \
procedures of finding the normal vector and the projection tensor of a \
hypersurface which is deformed from the spherical shell ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"r", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["R", "i"], "=", " ", "constant"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"d1a264da-8dbb-a541-ab11-3d44f2e5d307"],
 " in the unperturbed configuration. With those, we can apply the Israel \
junction conditions, i.e. the continuity of the intrinsic/ extrinsic \
curvature, to relate the two sides of an interface. Ref: ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Finn 1990",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1990MNRAS.245...82F/abstract"], 
    None},
  ButtonNote->
   "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1990MNRAS.245...82F/abstract"],
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 ", ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Poisson: A relativist\[CloseCurlyQuote]s toolkit",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://www.amazon.com/Relativists-Toolkit-Mathematics-Black-Hole-\
Mechanics/dp/0521537800"], None},
  ButtonNote->
   "https://www.amazon.com/Relativists-Toolkit-Mathematics-Black-Hole-\
Mechanics/dp/0521537800"],
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9628132451615753`*^9, 3.9628132505441017`*^9}, {
  3.9843997055432854`*^9, 3.984399707931595*^9}, {3.9844119955713444`*^9, 
  3.984412068689184*^9}, {3.9844220797940216`*^9, 3.9844222194085217`*^9}, {
  3.9844222503166885`*^9, 3.98442226443721*^9}, {3.9844223160432453`*^9, 
  3.9844223347262287`*^9}, {3.9845212582199154`*^9, 3.984521518536009*^9}, {
  3.984521567681612*^9, 3.9845216348814964`*^9}, {3.9845217993505573`*^9, 
  3.9845218145652027`*^9}, {3.984522383881405*^9, 3.9845224409971657`*^9}},
 Background->GrayLevel[
  0.85],ExpressionUUID->"ea7c1771-cfaf-a949-872f-d5a7c4fb7a74"],

